[
  {
    "claim": "Marketplace asks for new-generation GPUs reprice much more often and in bigger steps than the rest of their class, so their q10-q90 intervals should be wider.",
    "rationale": "A marketplace ask is set by many independent hosts who undercut or withdraw offers whenever they like. Most of the remaining loss sits in B300, H200 and B200 marketplace asks, and nearly all of it comes on days the price moved. The pooled class distribution leans on calmer A100 and H100 asks, so it underestimates the size of new-generation moves. This widens the interval for this one class only. H13 widened every class and failed because it also widened calm series.",
    "edits": [
      {"op": "width", "class": "marketplace_ask", "scale": 1.4}
    ]
  },
  {
    "claim": "Committee-set rate cards change rarely and in planned steps, so pooled change distributions make their intervals too wide.",
    "rationale": "Hyperscaler list prices and neocloud rate cards are repriced a few times a year by a pricing team, not continuously by a market. Borrowing from the class mixes in occasional large re-cards, which inflates the interval on the many days nothing happens. Narrowing only these slow classes cuts loss on flat days and leaves the volatile marketplace and spot classes alone. H14 narrowed every class at once, which is a different test.",
    "edits": [
      {"op": "width", "class": "hyperscaler_list", "scale": 0.6},
      {"op": "width", "class": "neocloud_rate_card", "scale": 0.7}
    ]
  },
  {
    "claim": "Prices are cut quickly and raised slowly: as Blackwell supply ramps up, downward steps are bigger than history shows and upward steps are smaller.",
    "rationale": "In late 2026, B200 and B300 capacity is coming online and hosts compete by undercutting each other, while raising a posted ask loses renters straight away. Both one-sided tests failed in the direction this predicts: stretching the upside hurt (H19), so the upside is already too wide, and shrinking the downside hurt (H20), so the downside is too narrow. Changing both tails together in opposite directions has not been tried.",
    "edits": [
      {"op": "tail", "down": 1.25, "up": 0.85}
    ]
  },
  {
    "claim": "Price changes come in campaigns of same-direction steps, so the last-step-direction state should be weighted much more sharply than 0.3.",
    "rationale": "H09 was the largest single gain (+0.052), which suggests momentum is real and the current weight still lets too many opposite-direction past days dilute it. A provider that starts cutting prices usually keeps cutting until it reaches a competitor's price, and one that starts raising does the same. Lowering the weight on past days with a different last direction to 0.1 makes the change distribution reflect the current campaign.",
    "edits": [
      {"op": "condition", "feature": "last_dir", "weight": 0.1}
    ]
  },
  {
    "claim": "A deviation from the 30-day median caused by a provider joining or leaving reverses, while a deviation without one persists, so the two states should condition together.",
    "rationale": "A posted median jumps when a cheap or expensive provider enters or leaves the sample. That is a composition effect, and it undoes itself when the sample changes back. A deviation with no change in providers is a real repricing and tends to stick. H10 tested provider_change alone and could not use this, because the effect only shows up together with deviation. This makes deviation sharper and adds a soft provider_change condition so the forecast can tell the two cases apart.",
    "edits": [
      {"op": "condition", "feature": "provider_change", "weight": 0.5},
      {"op": "condition", "feature": "deviation", "weight": 0.15}
    ]
  },
  {
    "claim": "New-generation series have too little history of their own, so changes borrowed from their class should count for much more than 20 observations.",
    "rationale": "B300 and many H200 and B200 listings are only a few months old. Their own change history is short and dominated by a handful of launch-period steps, which is exactly where the loss is concentrated. Class pooling (H04) helped. Raising its weight tests whether the right amount of borrowing is larger, so young series are shrunk harder toward how their class has behaved.",
    "edits": [
      {"op": "set", "field": "pool_weight", "value": 50}
    ]
  },
  {
    "claim": "The cheapest-offer price on an aggregator flickers when one unusually cheap listing appears for a day and then vanishes, so this class alone should forecast from a 2-day median level.",
    "rationale": "An aggregator's cheapest price is set by a single listing. A one-day spot from one host moves it and then reverses, so the last print overstates the price change. H22 smoothed every class over 3 days and failed badly because real step series should stay on their last value. This applies the shortest smoothing allowed only to the class where single listings cause flicker.",
    "edits": [
      {"op": "anchor", "class": "aggregator_cheapest", "days": 2}
    ]
  },
  {
    "claim": "A price that has been flat for a long time while the number of posted offers shrinks is about to step. Neither signal works alone, but together they mark a stale price under real supply pressure.",
    "rationale": "Hosts pull offers before they reprice, and a falling offer count on a price that has just changed is only churn. Mild conditioning on both states focuses the forecast on past moments where a long flat stretch and thinning supply happened together. H06 and H07 each tested one of these signals alone, and both were diluted by cases without the other.",
    "edits": [
      {"op": "condition", "feature": "since_change", "weight": 0.5},
      {"op": "condition", "feature": "offer_trend", "weight": 0.5}
    ]
  }
]

